+  // Lobium: seeded WebGL pixel farbling (unlinkability). Perturb EVERY RGBA/UNSIGNED_BYTE readback -
+  // the fingerprinting-canonical layout - so two profiles on the same real GPU get distinct-but-stable
+  // WebGL pixel hashes instead of an identical, linkable one. Keyed on seeds.webgl with a FLIPPED-ROW
+  // mapping (readPixels rows are bottom-up) against the drawing-buffer height, so it stays coherent
+  // with the top-down WebGL toDataURL/toBlob snapshot farble.
+  //
+  // The gate keys on the EFFECTIVE pixel geometry, never on the raw pack enums. Two bypasses lived in
+  // the old condition, and both returned the TRUE GPU pixels:
+  //
+  //   * `pack.row_length == 0` treated a no-op as a reason to stop. PACK_ROW_LENGTH == 0 and
+  //     PACK_ROW_LENGTH == width both mean "row stride == width", so one pixelStorei call that
+  //     changes not a single byte switched farbling off. Measured on the shipping binary: 4,072 bytes
+  //     of a 64x64 textured scene came back different. The stride is now derived from
+  //     (row_length ? row_length : width), so a no-op still farbles and a genuinely strided read
+  //     farbles correctly - the kernel writes `width` px per row and steps over the gap via row_bytes.
+  //     PACK_ALIGNMENT only rounds each row up; it is folded into row_bytes and never gates.
+  //
+  //   * `framebuffer == nullptr` skipped every user-bound FBO. Rendering the same scene into an FBO
+  //     and reading THAT back handed over the unfarbled pixels. Because the key is the absolute
+  //     drawing-buffer coordinate, a default-framebuffer read and a same-content FBO read now key
+  //     IDENTICALLY, so a page that does both and diffs them still sees agreement.
+  //
+  // Left untouched, deliberately: opaque WebXR framebuffers; genuine PARTIAL reads
+  // (PACK_SKIP_PIXELS/PACK_SKIP_ROWS != 0 place pixels at a leading byte offset inside `data`, so
+  // farbling from `data` would hit the wrong bytes); and non-RGBA8 formats, which the 8-bit kernel
+  // cannot represent.
